Aerie-Purpose: Make navigator.keyboard.getLayoutMap() return a promise rejected with NotSupportedError while fingerprinting protection is on for the site of the page, so the physical keyboard layout (which gives away the user's region) is not exposed; navigator.keyboard.lock() and unlock() are untouched (issue #203).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: content/browser/keyboard_lock/keyboard_lock_service_impl.cc (GetKeyboardLayoutMap), third_party/blink/public/mojom/keyboard_lock/keyboard_lock.mojom (GetKeyboardLayoutMapStatus), third_party/blink/renderer/modules/keyboard/keyboard_layout.cc (GotKeyboardLayoutMap), content/browser/BUILD.gn
Aerie-Kind: hook

Upstream answers getLayoutMap() in the browser (KeyboardLockServiceImpl::GetKeyboardLayoutMap) with
the layout map of the focused page's window, and the renderer resolves the promise with it. The
map has 49 entries on a US keyboard and different key names on an AZERTY, QWERTZ or Dvorak
keyboard, so it shows the user's region and layout.

The browser decides, per request, after the permissions-policy check that upstream does first:
protection is on for the site of the top page
(ContentBrowserClient::HeliumNoiseEnabled for the origin of the outermost main frame: the pref,
the per-site HELIUM_NOISE setting and the HeliumNoise feature, the rule that decides whether a page
gets its noise tokens). Then the answer is a new status, GetKeyboardLayoutMapStatus::kNotSupported,
and the renderer rejects the promise with a NotSupportedError DOMException, the outcome in a
browser without the API (Firefox, Safari). Otherwise nothing changes and the real map comes back.
For a top page with an opaque origin (a sandboxed or data: page, which has no site to switch off)
the URL is empty and HeliumNoiseEnabled answers from the feature and the pref alone, so the map is
refused whenever protection is on (fail closed). Protection off, globally or for the site, or
--disable-features=HeliumNoise, gives back the real map. A frame of another site in the page gets
the answer of its top page.

The decision is Aerie's own pure code with unit tests (components/aerie_keyboard_layout). The
surface has no token and no derived value, so nothing is stored or sent to a renderer. The
layout map is a web-exposed value only: KeyboardEvent.key and code, keyboard.lock() and unlock(),
and input method detection are not changed.

